
# Task 14: Curve Fitting and Approximation

## 1. Objective
The aim is to fit a mathematical curve to given data points using SciPy.

## 2. Inputs
The original data is:

x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
y = [3, 5, 7, 9, 11]

## 3. Mathematical Operations
The program uses SciPy's curve_fit() function to determine the slope and intercept of a straight-line model.

## 4. Original Output
Slope = 2.0.
Intercept = 1.0.
Predicted value at x = 6 is 13.0.

## 5. Modified Input
The original y-values were changed to:

[4, 7, 10, 13, 16]

## 6. Observation
Changing the data points changes the fitted line and the predicted value.

## 7. Interpretation
Curve fitting helps identify mathematical relationships between variables and make predictions.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem
Curve fitting is used in scientific experiments, business forecasting and analysing trends.

## 9. Assumptions
The data follows an approximately linear relationship.

## 10. New Input Data
The modified data is:

x = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
y = [4, 7, 10, 13, 16]

## 11. Output
Slope = 3.0.
Intercept = 1.0.
Predicted value at x = 6 is 19.0.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem
The modified data produces a steeper fitted line and a higher predicted value, showing how curve fitting responds to changes in input data.
